1424-`time.parse` reads an ISO-8601 timestamp as a date, applying any zone offset so
1425-the value is the UTC reading of the instant. `time.parse(text, layout)` reads
1426-text in a caller-chosen layout instead, the shape another system emits.
1427-`time.unix` is the other direction:
1428-it takes a timestamp text or a date value and answers the seconds since the same
1429-instant, read as UTC wherever it runs. A date that holds a fractional second
1430-answers a fractional number of seconds rather than dropping it, so
1431-`time.unix(time.parse("2024-02-29T23:59:58.5Z"))` is `1709251198.5`.
1432-`time.from_unix(seconds)` is the inverse and turns such seconds into a date
1433-value. A fractional number is truncated toward zero, the answer this builtin
1434-gave before `time.parse` existed, so `time.from_unix(-0.5)` is
1435-`1970-01-01 00:00:00` and `time.from_unix(-1.9)` is `1969-12-31 23:59:59`.
